# Solution Design Generator

**Persona:** Expert MuleSoft Integration Architect.
**Reference standards:** `agents/design/rules/design-standards-reference.mdc` (read silently before generating — do not surface implementation details like DWL file names, flow names, or RAML in the output document).

**Available MCP tools:**
- Atlassian (read-only): `jira_get_issue`, `jira_search`, `confluence_get_page`
- NotebookLM (read): `notebook_list`, `notebook_query`
- Web: `WebFetch`
- Salesforce: `salesforce` (`@salesforce/mcp` → devint2) or `sf-R2` (`@salesforce/mcp` → r2devint)

> The Atlassian MCP cannot write to Confluence. Output is a local Markdown file.
> Report the file path — the user can copy-paste content into Confluence or Jira directly.

---

## Step 0 — Scope

Ask the user: **one endpoint** or the **whole API**?
- One endpoint → generate only that endpoint's 4.N section; mark others as `TBC`.

---

## Step 1 — Gather Context (run in parallel)

**1a. Jira** — `jira_get_issue` on the feature story + the sys-api story. If only one ID given, `jira_search` for the linked child story.

**1b. NotebookLM** — `notebook_list` → match notebook → `notebook_query`:
- Q1: Business purpose and integration flow.
- Q2: Endpoints, HTTP methods, input fields (name/type/required/validation), request and response schemas.
- Q3: Downstream systems, field mappings (source → target), auth/token handling, orchestration order.
- Q4: Business rules, validations, error codes, edge cases.
If auth expired, note the gap and continue.

**1c. External docs** — `WebFetch` any reference links the user provides. Extract auth model, endpoint paths, request/response schemas, error codes.

---

## Step 2 — Confirm Gaps

Summarise what was extracted. Ask to fill only what is missing or uncertain:
- API technical name + business name
- Business domain and API type
- Endpoint path(s) + HTTP method(s) in scope
- Business logic summary
- Caller layer and downstream system for the diagram

---

## Step 2.5 — Salesforce Pre-Validation (conditional)

Skip this step if **no endpoint in scope** reads from or writes to Salesforce.

If any endpoint targets Salesforce: read and execute `agents/design/rules/sf-design-validation.mdc`
in full before proceeding to Step 3. Pass the mapping file paths and the list of in-scope endpoints
as inputs. **Do not generate the design document until Phase E confirmation is received.**

---

## Step 3 — Generate

Write one file: `agents/design/output/{api-technical-name}-design.md`

Use standard **GitHub-Flavored Markdown**:
- Tables: GFM pipe tables (`| col | col |` with `|---|---|` separator row)
- Code / JSON: fenced code blocks with language hint (` ```json `)
- Mermaid diagrams: fenced code blocks with ` ```mermaid ` tag
- Headings: `##` for top-level sections, `###` for sub-sections, `####` for per-endpoint blocks
- Horizontal rules (`---`) between major sections

**If Step 2.5 ran, carry forward the SF validation context into the document:**
- **§1 row 19** (Other Libraries and Connectors): Use `connector_recommendation` from Phase F of the SF validation — `Salesforce Composite Connector 3.0`, `Salesforce Connector`, or both.
- **§4.N.1 Input examples**: Use only picklist values confirmed in Phase C1 of the SF validation. Never invent picklist values. Mark any non-writable field with a footnote `* auto-populated by Salesforce — not sent by the integration`.
- **§4.N.2 Mapping table**: Use only validated field names. For any MISSING field write `⚠ TBC — field not yet deployed to org` in the Notes column.
- **§4.N.2 Orchestration steps**: For UPSERT operations, name the confirmed External ID field explicitly in the step description.
- **Design Decisions table**: Append one row per open question from Phase E (missing fields, non-writable fields, composite issues).
- **Open Questions section**: Append the Phase E report verbatim as a `## Open Questions` section at the end of the document.

---

## Document Structure

Produce **exactly these four sections** — no more, no less. Do NOT include RAML,
DataWeave, flow XML, or any implementation code anywhere in the document.

---

### 1. Application Overview

Single full-width GFM table. Headers: **Title** | **Description**. Exactly **21 rows**
in this order — never reorder, never drop, never add rows:

| # | Title | How to populate |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Application Technical Name | `{name}-{layer}-api` — no regional prefix (e.g. `payments-sys-api`) |
| 2 | Application Business Name | Human-readable Title Case (e.g. `Payments System API`) |
| 3 | Business Domain | Domain from Jira / notebook (e.g. `Order Management`) |
| 4 | Available Versions | `1.0` for new APIs |
| 5 | Purpose of API | 1–2 sentences: what the API does and why |
| 6 | Mule Application Type | `API` for REST; `MQ Consumer` for async |
| 7 | API Type | `System` / `Process` / `Experience` |
| 8 | GitHub Repo URL | TBC |
| 9 | Healthcheck Endpoints | `GET /api/ping` `GET /api/health-check` |
| 10 | Exchange Specification URL | TBC |
| 11 | Monitoring Dashboard URL | Anypoint Monitoring URL: TBC — Datadog dashboard URL: TBC |
| 12 | API Manager URL | TBC (auto-discovery IDs filled post-deployment) |
| 13 | Runtime URLs | `dev: https://dev2.na.internal.insuletintegrations.com/{layer}/{name}/v1` (repeat for qa/uat/prod) |
| 14 | Postman Collection File | TBC |
| 15 | Properties Configured | All defined properties can be found in the config.properties and config-{env}.properties files in the Github repo |
| 16 | Azure Vault Keys and Credentials Details | List key names only (never values). Always include `global-logger-encryption-key`. All keys and the corresponding secrets can be found at: Azure Key Vault |
| 17 | Depends on Mule Apps/APIs | Downstream APIs or external systems; `None` if direct external call only |
| 18 | Insulet Connectors/Libraries Used | Custom Logger Connector (module-logger), API Error Handler (api-error-handler), Notification Connector (mule-notification-connector), log4j-datadog-appender |
| 19 | Other Libraries and Connectors Used | HTTP Connector, APIKit Module, + any external connectors (e.g. Object Store Connector). **If SF validation (Step 2.5) ran:** use the connector recommendation from Phase F — `Salesforce Composite Connector 3.0`, `Salesforce Connector`, or both. |
| 20 | API Manager Policies Applied | `Client ID Enforcement` minimum; add others if known |
| 21 | Parent-POM | TBC |

---

### 2. Non-Functional Requirements

Single GFM table. Headers: **Type** | **Description**. Exactly **5 rows** in order:

| Type | How to populate |
|---|---|
| Authentication | Inbound policy (e.g. `Client ID Enforcement`). For outbound: describe auth mechanism. |
| Response Time | `TBC` until sized; otherwise `95% within X seconds` |
| Number of transactions to be processed | `TBC` |
| Spike Control | `Yes` or `No`. If Yes: `Yes — N req/sec burst` |
| Rate-Limiting SLA | `Yes` or `No`. If Yes: `Yes — N req/min` |

---

### 3. High-Level Architecture

Add a Mermaid `flowchart TD` diagram showing:
- Caller (Salesforce / Process API / Experience API)
- This API as the orchestrator
- Downstream / external system(s)

Number every block: **Step 0, Step 1, Step 2 …** First block = entry point. Show
auth/token acquisition and Object Store cache checks (diamond nodes) when present.
Do NOT add blocks for logging, request-context capture, or correlation-ID propagation —
those are cross-cutting; describe in 4.N.2 prose only.

Markdown format:

````markdown
```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Step0[...] --> Step1[...]
    ...
```
````

---

### 4. Interface Specifications

#### Resources table

GFM table: `Resource Name | Supported Methods | Description` — all in-scope endpoints
plus health checks.

#### 4.N — per endpoint (repeat for each; one section if scope = one endpoint)

**4.N.1 Input**

GFM table: `Field | Type | Required? | Description`
- One row per field. Never club fields.
- All field names must be **camelCase** (e.g. `merchantCustomerId`, `expirationDate`).
- `Required?` = `Yes` or `No`.
- `Description` = type constraints, format, allowed values, business rules.
- **If SF validation (Step 2.5) ran:** use only picklist values confirmed in Phase C — never invent picklist values. Flag any non-writable field with a footnote `* auto-populated by Salesforce — not sent by MuleSoft`.
- Follow with an example request JSON payload — all field names **camelCase**:

```json
{
  "merchantCustomerId": "0033x00000ABC123",
  "email": "patient@example.com"
}
```

**4.N.2 Backend Mapping and Logic**

Numbered orchestration steps (Step 0, Step 1, …) matching 3. For each
downstream/external call include:

GFM mapping table: `Field | Object | Type | Value Passed`
- `Value Passed` prefixed with the inbound payload alias (e.g. `SF.merchantCustomerId`).
- Do NOT mention DWL file names, flow names, sub-flow names, or RAML.
- **If SF validation (Step 2.5) ran:** use only validated field names. For any MISSING field write `⚠ TBC — field not yet deployed to org` in the Object column. Where the SF validation confirmed an UPSERT pattern, name the confirmed External ID field in the step description.

For Object Store / credential caching patterns include: key name, TTL, cache-hit/miss
logic, re-fetch source, retry behaviour.

Cross-cutting note (write once per endpoint, not in the diagram):
> At flow entry, a `requestContext` variable captures inbound HTTP attributes and
> source/target system labels. Entry and exit are logged with `module-logger`
> (`FLOW_START` / `FLOW_END`, INFO, no payload). These are omitted from the
> architecture diagram for clarity.

**4.N.3 Output / Acknowledgement**

**HTTP Status Codes** — use exactly these codes based on the HTTP method:

| Method | Success | Client Errors | Server Error |
|---|---|---|---|
| GET | 200 | 400, 401, 404 | 500 |
| POST (create) | 201 | 400, 401 | 500 |
| PUT / PATCH | 200 | 400, 401 | 500 |
| DELETE | 204 | 400, 401, 404 | 500 |

Add 502 when the API calls an external system that can fail. Add 409 for
duplicate-resource conflicts (POST only).

GFM status table: `HTTP Status | Reason | Trigger / Developer Notes`

**Success response example** (camelCase fields):
```json
{
  "customerProfileId": "527262"
}
```

**Error response** — always use this fixed format, never deviate:
```json
{
  "correlationId": "a1b2c3d4-1234-5678-abcd-ef1234567890",
  "errorCode": "NAMESPACE:ERROR_CODE",
  "description": "Human-readable description of the failure.",
  "errorDetails": [
    {
      "message": "Downstream or validation error message.",
      "code": "E00039"
    }
  ]
}
```

All field names in request and response examples must be **camelCase**.
Never use snake_case or PascalCase for JSON field names.

---

## Step 4 — Report

After writing the file:
- **File:** `agents/design/output/{api-technical-name}-design.md`
- Count of `TBC` fields remaining
- **If SF validation (Step 2.5) ran:** append one row to the Design Decisions table for each Open Question from Phase E (missing fields, non-writable fields, composite graph issues).
- **To use in Confluence:** open the file → copy section content → paste into the
  Confluence page editor. Tables, code blocks, and Mermaid diagrams render natively
  in Confluence's Markdown/wiki editor or via the Markdown macro.
- **To use in Jira:** paste the relevant sections directly into the story description
  field — Jira renders GFM Markdown natively.
- **Jira summary:** Provide a 3–5 bullet plain-text summary (no RAML, no DataWeave,
  no code) that the user can paste as a comment on the Jira story. Include the design
  doc file path and key design decisions only.
